// PARKED 2026-08-15 (soak aug14f, ryouko reactive divergence_000003).
// OPEN — survives the kernel-result bottom-persist fix. Deterministic.
//
//   interp: ep0 [(0,-1),(1,1)]  epN [(1, 2N+1)]
//   jit:    ep0 [(0,-1),(1,1)]  epN [(0, prev),(1, 2N+1)]  <- extra
//
// The jit fires the result at the INJECTION cycle (offset 0) carrying
// the PREVIOUS epoch's result; the interp fires only at offset 1
// (the connect-propagated cycle). This is the refined core of what
// the 2026-08-15 README called "Family B": the aug14f fix pass split
// the old three-witness signature — reactive_guard_arm_epoch3 fell to
// the bottom-persist fix, while this cluster survives with FIVE
// witnesses sharing one shape:
//
//   * a RECURSIVE fn called with CONSTANT args (v0(i64:5)) whose
//     terminal select reads a schedule input in an arm the taken
//     path never takes (`select (v2 /? 42) {i64 as n => n, _ => in1}`)
//   * a `<-` connect target elsewhere in the program
//   * every epoch after the first: extra jit fire at offset 0 with a
//     stale value.
//
// Siblings (all still diverging on the fixed tree): hz0 aug14f
// reactive 000000 (= reactive_extra_stale_fire.gx, has the campaign
// original) and 000001 (rec v3(12,0)), ryouko aug14f reactive 000001
// (rec v1(11), bare `v6 <- in0`), aieka aug14f reactive 000004
// (rec v5(4,0), `+?` terminal). Lead: the interp is quiet at offset 0
// because the input is consumed only by an UNTAKEN arm; the compiled
// callee kernel's region counts it as a fired input and re-emits.
// schedule-v1: cap=128 events=1024; in1=i64:0
{let v4: i64 = {let rec v0 = |v1: i64| -> i64 select v1 {v2 if v2 <= i64:0 => select (v2 /? i64:42) {i64 as n => n, _ => in1}, v2 => v0(v2 - i64:1)}; let v3 = v0(i64:5); (i64:1 - (v3 * v3))}; let v5: Array<i64> = []; v5 <- in1 ~ array::push(v5, in1); let v6 = array::len(v5); ((v6 + v6) - v4)}
